WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
React Native builds directly on to the standard Apple tool chain rather than bypassing it.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.600
The framework automates the setup of a native iOS foundation,

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.900
utilizing the same build phases, dependency managers, and compilers used in traditional development.

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.400
Initializing a project generates an iOS folder containing a complete standard Xcode project.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.200
The operational divergence happens immediately,

6
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.900
attempting to build from the standard .xcode project file results in an immediate failure.

7
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.100
The .xc workspace file is the only valid entry point.

8
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.600
Every native dependency in React Native flows strictly through Cocoa Pods targets

9
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.100
which Xcode can only resolve from inside the workspace.

10
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:52.700
Understanding React Native on iOS requires deconstructing exactly how its tools use the standard Cocoa Pods in Xcode build phase pipelines to configure the environment.

11
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.500
The React Native pod file functions as the central nervous system of the native layer,

12
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.600
controlling both the core framework internals and every third-party community library.

13
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.200
At the top of this file, the minimum deployment target is defined as iOS 14.0.

14
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.000
This value is required because the underlying C++ rendering engine, fabric,

15
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.700
and the JavaScript interface APIs depend on system methods introduced in iOS 14.

16
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.500
This schematic visualizes the use native module's Ruby macro,

17
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.900
which eliminates the need for manual native dependency linking.

18
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.900
It automatically scans the node module's directory,

19
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.900
locating .podspec files hidden inside NPM packages.

20
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:39.200
It then injects these resolved specs directly into your Xcode workspace configuration during a pod install.

21
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.000
Below that lies a secondary macro, use React Native.

22
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.800
This command injects the core React architecture,

23
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.600
passing specific arguments to enable the fabric renderer for native components and the Hermes JavaScript engine.

24
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.600
This reliance on macros explains the current limitations regarding Swift package manager in React Native development.

25
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.800
Swift package manager currently lacks the robust post-install hook systems required to patch deployment targets

26
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.500
and propagates specific C++ compiler flags across dozens of subspecs simultaneously.

27
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.900
Because React Native uses these specific Ruby macros to configure the build,

28
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.300
Cocoa Pods remains the required path for anything bridging to JavaScript.

29
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.700
Navigating to the project's root entry file,

30
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.000
we find app delegate using the .MM extension.

31
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.700
This objective C++ extension is a technical requirement for the modern runtime rather than a legacy artifact.

32
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.800
This split plane diagram represents the boundary where the standard iOS lifecycle interfaces

33
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.400
with the React Native C++ runtime.

34
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.200
Pure Swift cannot be used at this root level because it lacks the ability to directly import the C++ headers

35
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.300
required to initialize JSI and turbo modules.

36
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.000
Individual custom modules and view controllers can still be written in Swift,

37
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.600
provided the initial application root remains an objective C++.

38
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.500
In modern React Native versions, this file uses RCT app delegate as its base class.

39
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.000
This class reads the project's configuration and signals new architecture features to the runtime.

40
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.500
Developers avoid manually toggling architecture flags inside this file.

41
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.300
Those properties are controlled by the pod file macros,

42
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:22.100
which propagate through compiler preprocessor defines like RCT new arc enabled.

43
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:28.400
The .MM extension is a deliberate border wall that enforces proper C++ runtime initialization

44
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.100
while protecting the developer's Swift code.

45
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.200
The final structural modification happens inside the Xcode build phases

46
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.600
where React Native injects a custom execution script.

47
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.800
Execution order here is strict.

48
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:48.500
The bundle React Native code and images phase must run before Xcode reaches the copy bundle resources phase

49
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.600
to ensure the generated assets exist before they are embedded.

50
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.200
This logic flow chart illustrates how the script branches the application's behavior

51
00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.400
based on the build configuration.

52
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.800
In the debug path, the script initiates a local Metro bundle server on your machine.

53
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.400
In this mode, the app dynamically fetches the JavaScript payload over HTTP from local host,

54
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.200
enabling fast refreshing during development.

55
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.500
In the release path, the script bypasses the local server

56
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.100
and commands the bundler to compile a static main.js bundle file.

57
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:25.900
This static bundling ensures production IPA files are entirely self-contained

58
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.900
and require no local server connection to execute.

59
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.500
To manage environment variables for scenarios like a staging server,

60
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.600
you must extend this configuration structure manually.

61
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.000
This process involves duplicating the release configuration in Xcode

62
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.100
and creating a custom scheme inherited.exe config file for the new environment.

63
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:53.000
When creating these custom configurations, you must include the pods.exe config at the top of the file.

64
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.600
Cocoa pods writes its linker flags there and omitting that include breaks the link phase.

65
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.200
These injected scripts and configuration files are the mechanisms

66
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:08.500
that transform a standard iOS target into an environment capable of executing JavaScript.

67
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.700
Because this architecture relies on deep automation,

68
00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.800
linking Ruby, Xcode, and Node, pipeline desynchronization

69
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.300
causes the majority of build failures.

70
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:24.800
The most common symptom is the terminal log error, undefined symbol, underscore RCT bridge.

71
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.200
This specific error indicates the linker and not find react native symbols,

72
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.100
typically caused by opening the .exe project file instead of the workspace

73
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:38.100
or neglecting to run pod install after adding a dependency.

74
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.900
A more hidden danger occurs when modifying new architecture flags in the pod file

75
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.100
without clearing the build environment.

76
00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.300
Xcode caches compiled pod targets in the system's derived data folder

77
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.300
to speed up compile times.

78
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.000
Changing compiler defines without flushing this derived data cache

79
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.900
causes Xcode to link stale binaries built with previous flags,

80
00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.300
leading to silent runtime crashes.

81
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:05.800
Committing the pod file.lock file ensures environment parity across a team,

82
00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.200
guaranteeing every developer and CI machine builds with the same native code.

83
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.600
The system functions as a standard C++ bound Cocoa pods workspace,

84
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.800
one that iOS engineers already have the tools to master.

